/**
@page fem_time_dg_elastodynamics Energy-corrected DG(1) elastodynamics

Implements report.pdf, Sections 7, 9 and 17, equations (28)-(35), (54)-(58).
The selected baseline is p=1, alpha=4, a fixed spatial trial space and
state-independent, time-independent, nonsingular consistent reference mass.
The only global forward/adjoint unknowns are displacement coefficients.

@section time_dg_configuration Configuration

Give each displacement component two temporal bases with TSA_time_level_per_type.
Set time_dg=true on every participating spatial bulk, reference-traction,
Dirichlet and ghost IWG. Use TIME_DG_MASS on the active volume mesh sets with
 time_continuity=true, Density, and optionally Thickness, InitialDisplacement,
InitialVelocity and InitialTime. InitialTime defaults to zero; absent initial
fields are zero. Do not also add TIME_CONTINUITY_DOF to this displacement field.
All active phases need their own inertia configuration and positive density.

Time_DG_Operators generates the augmented temporal operators from Gauss
integration and incoming endpoint collocation. It inverts only a fixed 2x2
temporal matrix. No spatial mass matrix or inverse is constructed.

For a slab [t_n,t_(n+1)] with xi=(t-t_n)/dt, q_h=(1-xi)Q_0+xi Q_1.
The two known incoming traces q_minus and v_minus are the preceding slab's
outgoing displacement and reconstructed velocity. The augmented temporal
matrices are H=[[1/2,1/2],[1,0]], G=[[0,1],[3,1]], e=[1,4]^T,
A=H^{-1}G=[[3,1],[-3,1]] and b=H^{-1}e=[4,-2]^T. Reconstruction is
 V_0=(3 Q_0+Q_1-4 q_minus)/dt,
 V_1=(-3 Q_0+Q_1+2 q_minus)/dt.
For f=f_internal-f_external, assemble the displacement-only equations
 R_0=M(V_1-v_minus)+integral_[t_n,t_(n+1)] f(q_h(t),t) dt,
 R_1=M(3 V_0+V_1-4 v_minus)+dt f(Q_0,t_n).
These equations specify the implemented baseline independently of whether the
local report.pdf reference is distributed with the source tree. Density and
thickness must be finite positive scalars with no solution-DOF dependence;
spatial and design-variable dependence is permitted. Arbitrary callback time
dependence cannot be inferred automatically and is excluded by the contract.

The spatial wrapper sums the two ordinary temporal test blocks, extracting
the spatial test operator by partition of unity. It places the integrated
constant weak test in row 0. It evaluates the spatial force again at the
incoming endpoint and places it in row 1. The endpoint force is repeated with
the existing time quadrature weights, whose sum supplies exactly delta_t;
it is not multiplied by an extra delta_t. Trial temporal factors remain in
the consistent Jacobian, including material and geometric stiffness terms.
Both leader and follower rows are transformed for ghost contributions.
All evaluation points and material/property caches are restored afterward.
The spatial force rule uses six temporal Gauss points, independently of the
two temporal displacement bases. Finite quadrature is not an exact nonlinear
energy integration for arbitrary constitutive laws.

The TIME_DG_MASS IWG integrates the condensed inertia/history using spatial
quadrature on the incoming time sideset. Coefficient ordering is field,
temporal basis, then spatial basis, matching MORIS interpolation/assembly.
The coupled slab matrix is nonsymmetric and needs a compatible solver.

@section time_dg_isv ISV lifecycle and geometry derivatives

Cluster_ISV_Data contains a separate persistent Cluster_Time_History bank.
Legacy reset() clears assembly scratch only. Named velocity snapshots are
staged/committed once, after nonlinear solve completion, using freshly
extracted converged coefficients. Failed/aborted solves do not reach commit.
Starting a new forward trajectory clears the bank; starting an adjoint sweep
clears reverse carries while preserving the immutable forward trajectory.

Velocity is stored in its spatial interpolation coefficient representation,
not frozen as sampled physical values. Each quadrature evaluation interpolates
those coefficients at the current point. This is essential for the geometry
FD partials: interpolation changes when cut-cell integration coordinates are
perturbed. Storing sampled velocities and holding them fixed would omit this
part of the shape derivative. No Newton-trial derivative cache is retained.
The fixed spatial coefficient numbering must be maintained during a trajectory;
mesh adaptation, redistribution or changing quadrature needs explicit history
transfer and is outside this implementation.

Initial data is sampled at spatial interpolation nodes. Spatially varying
initial-data callbacks must use the IP geometry interpolator's valx(), because
those nodes need not lie in the current IG subcell. The supplied examples use
fixed reference initial fields independent of the ADVs. Design-dependent
prescribed initial fields require their own initial-state derivative terms.

@section time_dg_adjoint Exact displacement adjoint and local history carry

For DG(1), with Q_n=(Q_n0,Q_n1), the outgoing velocity is
 v_out_n=(-3 Q_n0+Q_n1+2 q_minus_n)/delta_t_n.
Consequently the eliminated residual depends on Q_n, Q_(n-1), and Q_(n-2).
The existing previous-slab Jacobian path assembles B_n=dR_n/dQ_(n-1):
 B_n,ab = rho[-(Ghat*b)_a*r_b/delta_t_n
                  -ehat_a*A_1b/delta_t_(n-1)] N_space^T N_space,
where r=(0,1). The remaining dependency is
 C_n,ab = rho*ehat_a*b_1*r_b/delta_t_(n-1) N_space^T N_space.
Thickness multiplies both expressions when specified.

MORIS's backward solve receives B_(n+1)^T lambda_(n+1) plus
C_(n+2)^T lambda_(n+2). The latter is a named local quadrature covector carried
through cluster ISV data. add_adjoint_history is called once after the full
slab adjoint is available, including all subsystem solves. It adds the delayed
term before projection through the equation object's interpolation map.
The carry stores all objective RHS columns and uses explicit element/point
indices; quadrature weights are applied once during assembly.
There is no velocity adjoint global solve, and no frozen-history approximation.
The first slab uses prescribed data rather than fictitious previous coefficients.

The current and previous state Jacobians and history reverse actions are
analytical. Material and geometry residual/IQI partials use MORIS's existing
semi-analytical quadrature FD infrastructure in the supplied examples. Local
FD evaluations do not mutate the committed history or reverse carries.

The supported example objectives are displacement/volume criteria. An ordinary
FI gradt() remains the displacement derivative; it must not be interpreted as
the reconstructed DG velocity. A velocity-dependent objective requires an IQI
that differentiates reconstruction and history explicitly.

@section time_dg_verification Verification sources and limits

UT_FEM_Time_DG covers temporal elimination, spatial weak/collocation row
assembly and current FD tangents, previous-slab FD tangents including velocity
reconstruction, persistent-history reset/rollback, delayed reverse actions
with multiple RHS columns, multislab density/stiffness-parameter adjoints on
unequal time slabs, and the conservative scalar energy identity.
The forward cantilever EXA in projects/EXA/structure/dynamics/Time_DG_Cantilever
supplies linear/StVK manufactured solutions. Its separate sensitivity example
in projects/EXA/optimization/Time_DG_Cantilever_Sensitivity supplies linear/StVK
cut-height/density adjoint-versus-FD sweeps on one/two MPI ranks.

The report's nonlinear energy identity assumes a potential consistent with
the assembled spatial forces and adequate temporal quadrature. Nonconvexity
can make the potential jump remainder negative. No unconditional energy decay
or exact angular-momentum conservation is claimed. Contact, changing active
spaces, rate-dependent/stateful constitutive laws and state-dependent mass
require additional history and derivative treatment. Boundary/stabilization
energy must be included when assessing the energy of a weakly clamped model.
*/
